This version keeps two pointers, one from each side. They move toward the middle and swap values that are on the wrong side of the pivot. Hoare partition does not place the pivot at a final index, so the recursive ranges are split around the returned boundary.

For example, `[4, 2, 5, 1, 3]` can be divided around `3` into values that still need sorting on the left and right. Each later call works on a smaller range.

```java
class Solution {
    public int[] quickSort(int[] nums) {
        if (nums.length > 1) {
            quickSortRange(nums, 0, nums.length - 1);
        }
        return nums;
    }

    private void quickSortRange(int[] nums, int left, int right) {
        if (left >= right) {
            return;
        }

        int split = partition(nums, left, right);
        quickSortRange(nums, left, split);
        quickSortRange(nums, split + 1, right);
    }

    private int partition(int[] nums, int left, int right) {
        int pivot = nums[left + (right - left) / 2];
        int i = left - 1;
        int j = right + 1;

        while (true) {
            do {
                ++i;
            } while (nums[i] < pivot);

            do {
                --j;
            } while (nums[j] > pivot);

            if (i >= j) {
                return j;
            }

            int temp = nums[i];
            nums[i] = nums[j];
            nums[j] = temp;
        }
    }
}
```

The average running time is `O(n log n)`, but it can reach `O(n^2)` with unbalanced splits. Average recursion space is `O(log n)`. Duplicate values are handled because the pointer checks stop when they reach the pivot range.

I used the last value as the pivot. After partitioning, every value on the left is smaller or equal, and every value on the right is larger. The same work is then repeated for both parts.

The array is sorted in place, so the input changes. An empty array or a one-element array returns immediately.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> quickSort(vector<int>& nums) {
        if (nums.size() < 2) {
            return nums;
        }

        sortRange(nums, 0, static_cast<int>(nums.size()) - 1);
        return nums;
    }

private:
    void sortRange(vector<int>& nums, int left, int right) {
        if (left >= right) {
            return;
        }

        int pivotIndex = partition(nums, left, right);
        sortRange(nums, left, pivotIndex - 1);
        sortRange(nums, pivotIndex + 1, right);
    }

    int partition(vector<int>& nums, int left, int right) {
        int pivot = nums[right];
        int smallerEnd = left;

        for (int i = left; i < right; ++i) {
            if (nums[i] <= pivot) {
                swap(nums[i], nums[smallerEnd]);
                ++smallerEnd;
            }
        }

        swap(nums[smallerEnd], nums[right]);
        return smallerEnd;
    }
};
```

Average time is `O(n log n)`, while a poor pivot choice can make it `O(n^2)`. The recursion uses `O(log n)` average space and `O(n)` in the worst case.

Recursion is replaced with a small stack of index ranges in this version. Each range is partitioned around the middle value, and the two resulting ranges are pushed for later processing. This avoids using the call stack.

The input vector is sorted in place. For `[]` and `[7]`, no range is pushed, so the original array is returned unchanged.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> quickSort(vector<int>& nums) {
        if (nums.size() < 2) {
            return nums;
        }

        vector<pair<int, int>> ranges;
        ranges.push_back({0, static_cast<int>(nums.size()) - 1});

        while (!ranges.empty()) {
            auto [left, right] = ranges.back();
            ranges.pop_back();

            int i = left;
            int j = right;
            int pivot = nums[left + (right - left) / 2];

            while (i <= j) {
                while (nums[i] < pivot) {
                    ++i;
                }
                while (nums[j] > pivot) {
                    --j;
                }

                if (i <= j) {
                    swap(nums[i], nums[j]);
                    ++i;
                    --j;
                }
            }

            if (left < j) {
                ranges.push_back({left, j});
            }
            if (i < right) {
                ranges.push_back({i, right});
            }
        }

        return nums;
    }
};
```

The average time is `O(n log n)` and the worst case is `O(n^2)`. The range stack uses `O(log n)` space on average, though it can grow to `O(n)` for poor splits.

Choosing a random pivot helps avoid consistently bad behavior when the input is already sorted or arranged in a simple pattern. The pivot is moved to the end, then a normal partition puts it between the two remaining ranges.

The random generator is kept inside the class. This still sorts the given array in place, and the returned reference is the same array.

```java
class Solution {
    private final java.util.Random random = new java.util.Random();

    public int[] quickSort(int[] nums) {
        if (nums.length > 1) {
            sortRange(nums, 0, nums.length - 1);
        }
        return nums;
    }

    private void sortRange(int[] nums, int left, int right) {
        if (left >= right) {
            return;
        }

        int randomIndex = left + random.nextInt(right - left + 1);
        swap(nums, randomIndex, right);

        int pivotIndex = partition(nums, left, right);
        sortRange(nums, left, pivotIndex - 1);
        sortRange(nums, pivotIndex + 1, right);
    }

    private int partition(int[] nums, int left, int right) {
        int pivot = nums[right];
        int storeIndex = left;

        for (int i = left; i < right; ++i) {
            if (nums[i] <= pivot) {
                swap(nums, i, storeIndex);
                ++storeIndex;
            }
        }

        swap(nums, storeIndex, right);
        return storeIndex;
    }

    private void swap(int[] nums, int first, int second) {
        int temp = nums[first];
        nums[first] = nums[second];
        nums[second] = temp;
    }
}
```

Expected time is `O(n log n)`, while the worst case remains `O(n^2)`. Expected recursion space is `O(log n)`. The random choice changes the split pattern, but it does not change the in-place nature of the code.

